+## 3.5 One Fat-Loss Phase, Not an Annual Cycle
+
+A correctly executed fat-loss phase should only have to happen once. Dieting every year to get in shape for summer is treated as evidence that the plan itself is flawed, not that the person lacks discipline.
+
+The failure pattern described is the bulk/cut loop: too much fat gained in the bulk, too much muscle lost in the cut, and the cut abandoned before it reaches a genuinely lean endpoint. The person cycles for years while looking roughly the same — never big, never lean, stuck in what is called "the messy middle."
+
+The stated resolution is a single, long, well-fed fat-loss phase taken to 10–15% body fat, followed by a structured reverse diet. After that, body composition is managed through lean-gaining phases with small pullbacks rather than through repeated full cuts.
+
+> "The person that gets leanest is the person that can stick to the deficit for the longest."
+
+**Evidence:** `DIRECT`  
+**Primary source:** “Why You Should Only Ever Have To Get Lean Once” — `CODEX/transcripts/why-you-should-only-ever-have-to-get-lean-once.txt`
