+### Rate of loss
+
+Target **0.5–1% of body weight per week**. Losing faster than this is described as "a one-way ticket to a rebound."
+
+The phase is expected to be long — roughly 20 weeks rather than 8 — because the deficit must be small enough to survive to the end. An 8-week attempt at a 20-week job is named as the reason most people never actually arrive.
+
+> "I would much rather you lost half a pound a week for 20 weeks and kept it off than lost three pounds a week for 10 weeks and put it back on again."
+
+**Evidence:** `DIRECT`  
+**Primary source:** “Why You Should Only Ever Have To Get Lean Once”
+
+### Lever order — activity first, calories last
+
+The deficit is built from activity before it is built from food:
+
+1. Start with a **small** calorie reduction, not a slashed intake. Opening a phase at 1,500 calories is called out by name as unsustainable — it leaves nowhere to go and nothing to reverse out of.
+2. Hold protein constant.
+3. Keep resistance training at 3–5 sessions per week, leading with big compounds (incline press, pulldowns, rows, squats, deadlifts). Isolation work is "the icing on the cake" and comes last in the session.
+4. **Increase cardio and steps over time** — these are the primary tools for widening the deficit.
+5. Only then reduce calories slowly, primarily from carbohydrates — reduced gradually, never taken to zero.
+
+The purpose of this ordering is to arrive at the goal on the highest food intake possible, because the size of the intake at the finish line determines whether a reverse diet is achievable.
+
+**Evidence:** `DIRECT`  
+**Primary source:** “Why You Should Only Ever Have To Get Lean Once”
+
+### Arriving in a reversible state
+
+Finishing the cut "destroyed" is treated as a failed cut even if the target weight was hit. Diet fatigue and hunger must be actively minimized on the way down, because a person who is cooked at the end will not execute the reverse — and the reverse is what protects the result.
+
+**Evidence:** `DIRECT`
+
