+### Concrete increment protocol
+
+A worked example is given from a finishing position of 2,000 calories, four training sessions per week, three 30-minute cardio sessions, and 10,000 steps per day.
+
+| Step | Change | Hold | Expected scale movement |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | **+400 calories** (~100 g carbohydrate, split 50 g pre-workout / 50 g post-workout) | 2 weeks | 1–2 lb |
+| 2 | **+200 calories** (~50 g carbohydrate) | 2 weeks | ~1 lb |
+| 3 | **+200 calories** (~50 g carbohydrate) | 2 weeks | ~1 lb |
+
+Net effect: **+800 calories over six weeks**, with total scale gain described as 3–5 lb, attributed to water and muscle rather than fat.
+
+Critically, **training, cardio, and steps are all held at their end-of-cut levels throughout the six weeks.** Only food increases. This is identified as the hardest part of the entire plan and the point at which most people fail — not because it is physically difficult, but because it lands exactly when the person is mentally finished with the diet, and often when a holiday is imminent.
+
+Reported outcome at the end of the reverse: appetite under control, better pumps and vascularity, improved training, still lean but visually fuller. From there the person may hold maintenance, drop back into another fat-loss phase from a much higher intake, or enter a lean-gaining phase.
+
+> "The exit strategy and the reverse diet is the most important part of this whole plan."
+
+**Evidence:** `DIRECT`  
+**Primary source:** “Why You Should Only Ever Have To Get Lean Once”
+
+### Reverse dieting before a cut
+
+A distinct case: a person who has yo-yoed repeatedly may stop responding to a deficit — reporting hunger at 1,800 calories with no fat loss, and rapid fat gain whenever intake rises. For these people a **12-week reverse diet is prescribed first**, before any fat-loss phase, even though their body composition does not appear to warrant it. Described as "one step back to then go two or three steps forward."
+
+Note: the transcript attributes the non-response to a slowed metabolism. The Codex records the *protocol* as `DIRECT` and the *mechanism* as requiring outside verification.
+
+**Evidence:** `DIRECT` (protocol) / `VERIFY_EXTERNALLY` (metabolic mechanism)  
+**Primary source:** “Why You Should Only Ever Have To Get Lean Once”
+
 ### Important nuance
 A reverse diet is not magic metabolic repair. Its practical value is behavioral control, structured calorie restoration, better training, and discovering maintenance without a rebound.
 
